      D1 — Trigger evaluation (spec Goals; AC-SIGNATURE-BUDGET-SEQUENCES) — (a)
      ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
      Chosen: one window summary + one decision on the fail path.

      Replace the escalation use of countTaskAttemptEvents (dumb attempt count) with:

        export const FIX_SIGNATURE_REPEAT_BUDGET = 2;   // Trigger R
        export const FIX_DISTINCT_SIGNATURE_BUDGET = 4; // Trigger D

      Stop using FIX_ATTEMPT_BUDGET for escalation. Prefer deleting the export if no
      remaining production callers after the edit; if tests or comments still need a
      name for "2", use FIX_SIGNATURE_REPEAT_BUDGET only (no dual-constant drift).
      Update MODULE_MAP accordingly.

      Window boundary (A24, unchanged source): lastResolvingResumeId(events, task)
      — walks the accounting stream (listAccountingEvents = durable+loose by id),
      tracks unresolved escalations, records the id of a resume that removes this
      task from that set. Ordinary resume that does not clear an escalation does
      not move the window. Returns 0 when never resolved.

      Single walk for the window's fail signatures (after the current fail is
      written, so the current signature is included):

        function listWindowFailSignatures(
          events: LooseEvent[], // or ReadonlyArray with payload
          task: string,
        ): FailureSignature[]
          // for each event with id > windowStart, task match, kind===attempt,
          // attributes.outcome===fail: read FailureSignature via readAttemptPayload
          // (exact kind + exact key). Pass attempts and VU are skipped.
          // Order: ascending event id.

      Decision (evaluate R then D; at most one fires):

        function decideFixBudgetEscalation(
          windowFails: FailureSignature[],
        ): { escalate: false }
          | { escalate: true; trigger: "R"; repeated: FailureSignature }
          | { escalate: true; trigger: "D"; distinctCount: number; signatures: FailureSignature[] }

        R: let cur = last signature in windowFails; if count of equals(cur) in
           windowFails >= FIX_SIGNATURE_REPEAT_BUDGET → R (repeated = cur).
        D: else if size of distinct (kind\0key) set >= FIX_DISTINCT_SIGNATURE_BUDGET
           → D.
        else no escalate.

      Equality: exact string equality on kind and on key (no case-fold).

      recordAttempt on fail path: write attempt → listAccountingEvents →
      listWindowFailSignatures → decideFixBudgetEscalation → maybe write escalation
      + formatEscalationMessage(trigger, …).

      formatEscalationMessage must branch on trigger:
        R: name repeated kind:key (not "after N attempts" as the unit).
        D: name four distinct unresolved failures (use the constant, not a hard-coded
           English "four" that can drift); may list signatures.
        Both: paused-pending-approval; task has not failed / replan owed.

      countTaskAttemptEvents may remain for non-escalation attemptCount display
      ("Attempt N for task") if useful; it must not gate escalation. Prefer leaving
      it with a comment that it is display/ordinal only, or narrowing its callers
      so a reader cannot mistake it for the budget.

      collectFailureSignatures: either become listWindowFailSignatures or call it —
      one definition of "fails in window", not two walks that can disagree.

      ── Self-closing Event trap (measured on C-LEGIBLE-FAILURE run-ledger) ──
      Production budget code does **not** re-parse ledger text with a
      `>…</Event>` regex. It walks LooseEvent[] from listLooseEvents /
      listLedgerEvents / listAccountingEvents, which use readGraceXmlArtifact
      (real XML tree). A self-closing `<Event … kind="resume" />` is already one
      complete node; the next attempt is a sibling — never swallowed.

      Binding mechanism (not "we remember"):
        (1) Escalation decision inputs are only LooseEvent[] / FailureSignature[]
            from the existing parse path — no new string scrape of run-ledger.xml.
        (2) lastResolvingResumeId continues to use event.kind on that stream.
        (3) Any test that uses archived ledgers as fixtures must load them via
            listLedgerEvents (or the recordAttempt API), never a hand-rolled Event
            regex. The six tasks at 2 fails / 2 distinct are optional goldens for
            a listWindowFailSignatures fixture test if cheap; not required if the
            sequence matrix already covers R and D.

